  "reasoning": "The patient presents with classic symptoms of an upper gastrointestinal disorder: intermittent burning stomach pain, gas, bloating, weight loss, and loss of appetite. Crucially, the presence of dark, tarry stools (melena) and blood in the vomit (hematemesis) points directly to active upper gastrointestinal bleeding. These combined findings are textbook indicators of a bleeding peptic ulcer. While gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) can cause burning pain and vomiting, it does not typically cause melena or significant hematemesis. Typhoid may cause abdominal pain and anorexia but lacks the characteristic burning pain and GI bleeding. Therefore, the facts firmly establish peptic ulcer disease as the correct diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]peptic ulcer disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
